WN.lb(37,{"mt_Jvg_r4yWaY":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"On a smooth ramp a skater starts still with {=g*10} J in her gravitational store. How many joules are in her kinetic store at the very bottom?","answer":"g*10","hint":"No friction: all of it swaps over.","explain":"All {=g*10} J moves from the gravitational store to the kinetic store.","params":{"g":[10,90]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"You charge a phone. Which description is right?","choices":["Energy is transferred electrically into the battery's chemical store","Electricity is stored as electricity in the phone","Energy is made inside the charger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Batteries store energy in chemicals.","explain":"The current carries energy (electrically) and it is stored in the battery's chemical store."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A sledge starts with 500 J. At the bottom of a rough hill it has {=k*10} J in its kinetic store and nothing in its gravitational store. How many joules went to thermal stores?","answer":"500-k*10","hint":"The total is still 500 J.","explain":"500 − {=k*10} = {=500-k*10} J went to thermal stores by heating.","params":{"k":[10,45]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Two skaters, 40 kg and 80 kg, roll from the same height on a smooth ramp. At the bottom…","choices":["they have the same speed","the heavier one is twice as fast","the lighter one is twice as fast"],"answer":0,"hint":"Twice the mass gives twice the energy, but also twice as much to speed up.","explain":"mgh = ½mv², so the mass cancels: v = √(2gh). Same height, same speed."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A hot cup of tea cools down on the table. Which store empties, and by which pathway?","choices":["Its thermal store, by heating the air and table (and some radiation)","Its chemical store, electrically","Its kinetic store, mechanically"],"answer":0,"hint":"What is the tea losing? How does a hot thing warm what is around it?","explain":"The tea's thermal store empties by heating the cooler air and table, and a little by infrared radiation."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A {m} kg trolley moves at {v} m/s. How many joules are in its kinetic store (½mv²)?","answer":"m*v*v/2","hint":"Square the speed first, then multiply by the mass and halve.","explain":"½ × {m} × {v}² = {=m*v*v/2} J.","params":{"m":[2,6],"v":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"How does energy from the Sun reach Earth through empty space?","choices":["By radiation (light and infrared)","By heating the air on the way","Electrically"],"answer":0,"hint":"Space has almost no air to heat.","explain":"Only radiation can cross empty space. The light and infrared then warm the ground's thermal store."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A stretched catapult is about to fire. Which store holds most of its energy?","choices":["Elastic potential","Kinetic","Chemical"],"answer":0,"hint":"Nothing is moving yet. What has been stretched?","explain":"Stretched or squashed things store energy in an elastic potential store. When it fires, that energy moves to the kinetic store of the stone."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A skater on a smooth ramp has {=t*10} J in total. Right now her kinetic store is {=k*10} J. How many joules are in her gravitational store?","answer":"t*10-k*10","hint":"No friction, so the total stays the same. Total − kinetic = ?","explain":"{=t*10} − {=k*10} = {=t*10-k*10} J in the gravitational store.","params":{"t":[20,60],"k":[5,50]},"where":"k<t"},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A bike skids to a stop. Its kinetic store is now empty. Where did that energy go?","choices":["Into thermal stores of the tyre, road and air, by heating","It was destroyed by the brakes","Back into the rider's chemical store"],"answer":0,"hint":"Feel a tyre just after a long skid.","explain":"Friction transfers the energy by heating. The tyre, road and air warm up a little. The total is the same; it is just spread out."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A {m} kg box is lifted {h} m onto a shelf. Using g = 10 N/kg, how much energy goes into its gravitational store (mgh)?","answer":"m*10*h","hint":"Multiply mass × 10 × height.","explain":"{m} × 10 × {h} = {=m*10*h} J.","params":{"m":[1,9],"h":[2,6]}},{"type":"order","id":"p5","prompt":"Put the energy journey in a torch in order.","items":["Chemical store of the battery","Transferred electrically by the current","Bulb heats up and radiates light","Thermal store of the surroundings"],"hint":"Start where the energy is stored before you switch on.","explain":"Chemical store → electrically → the bulb heats and gives out light → the light is absorbed, warming the surroundings."}],"explain":{"kid":["Think of energy like money in different bank accounts. The accounts are called stores. Moving things have a kinetic store, and high things have a gravitational store. Hot things have a thermal store, and food and fuel have a chemical store.","Energy moves from one store to another. A force can push it (mechanically), and a current can carry it (electrically). A hot thing can warm a cold one (heating), and light or sound can carry it away (radiation).","The total never changes. When a skater slows down on a rough ramp, the energy is not gone. It has gone into warm wheels, a warm ramp and warm air."],"adult":["The store model is bookkeeping: pick a start and an end, list which stores went up and which went down, and the changes must add to zero. Energy is conserved; it just becomes less useful as it spreads into thermal stores.","The stores to know are kinetic (½mv²), gravitational potential (mgh), elastic, thermal, chemical, nuclear, magnetic and electrostatic. The pathways are mechanical work, electrical work, heating and radiation.","A common slip is saying energy is 'used up' or 'lost'. Better: it is transferred to the surroundings, usually by heating, where we can't easily use it again."]},"familyTip":"Drop a ball from knee height and count how many bounces it makes. Each bounce is lower. Ask: where did the energy go? Feel the ball after many fast bounces on a hard floor.","keyIdea":"Energy is never made or destroyed. It sits in stores (kinetic, thermal, chemical and more) and moves between them mechanically, electrically, by heating or by radiation.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Where is the energy mostly stored? Drag each card into a store, or tap a card and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Kinetic","Gravitational","Chemical","Elastic"],"items":[["A rolling bowling ball",0],["A cyclist at top speed",0],["A book on a high shelf",1],["Water behind a dam",1],["A slice of toast",2],["A charged battery",2],["A stretched rubber band",3],["A squashed spring",3]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagenergy","caption":"Flagship lab: the skate ramp. Let the skater go and watch energy move between stores, but the total never change.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_2bnXrfS4Iq":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Two identical bulbs in one loop each have {h} V across them. What is the battery's voltage?","answer":"h*2","hint":"Around a loop the voltages add up to the battery's.","explain":"{h} + {h} = {=h*2} V.","params":{"h":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What does a current of 2 A mean?","choices":["2 coulombs of charge pass each second","2 joules of energy per charge","The battery has 2 volts"],"answer":0,"hint":"Current is a rate of flow.","explain":"1 amp = 1 coulomb per second, so 2 A means 2 coulombs of charge flow past each second."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Sam puts a voltmeter into the loop, in series with a bulb. What happens?","choices":["The bulb goes out, because a voltmeter hardly lets current through","The bulb gets brighter","Nothing changes"],"answer":0,"hint":"A voltmeter has a very high resistance.","explain":"A voltmeter has a huge resistance, so in the loop it almost stops the current. The bulb goes out."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"An ammeter in a loop reads {=a/10} A. A second ammeter, after two bulbs, is added. What does it read, in A?","answer":"a/10","hint":"In one loop, is current shared out or the same everywhere?","explain":"The same: {=a/10} A. Current is the same at every point in a single loop.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You add a second identical bulb to a one-bulb loop with the same battery. What happens?","choices":["Both bulbs are dimmer: each gets half the voltage and less current flows","Both stay just as bright","The first bulb gets brighter"],"answer":0,"hint":"The two bulbs share the battery's push.","explain":"The voltage is shared, and with more resistance the current falls, so both bulbs are dimmer."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is the best description of voltage?","choices":["The energy given to or taken from each unit of charge","How much charge flows each second","How fast the charges move"],"answer":0,"hint":"Its unit, the volt, is a joule per coulomb.","explain":"Voltage (potential difference) is energy per unit charge: 1 V = 1 J per coulomb."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A torch needs {v} V. Each cell gives 1.5 V. How many cells must be put in a row?","answer":"v/1.5","hint":"Divide the voltage needed by 1.5.","explain":"{v} ÷ 1.5 = {=v/1.5} cells.","params":{"v":[3,15]},"where":"v%3==0"}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"An ammeter before a bulb reads 0.4 A. What does an ammeter just after the bulb read?","choices":["0.4 A","Less than 0.4 A","0 A"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is charge used up in a bulb?","explain":"0.4 A. In a single loop the current is the same everywhere. The bulb takes energy from the charges, not the charges themselves."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A battery is made of {n} cells in a row, each 1.5 V. What is the battery's voltage?","answer":"n*1.5","hint":"Cells in a row add their voltages.","explain":"{n} × 1.5 = {=n*1.5} V.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How should a voltmeter be connected to measure the voltage of a bulb?","choices":["Across the bulb (in parallel)","In the loop next to the bulb (in series)","It doesn't matter"],"answer":0,"hint":"It compares the energy before and after the part.","explain":"Across the bulb, in parallel. It measures the energy each charge gives up between the two sides of the bulb."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A {v} V battery lights {b} identical bulbs in one loop. What is the voltage across each bulb?","answer":"v/b","hint":"The bulbs share the battery's voltage equally.","explain":"{v} ÷ {b} = {=v/b} V across each bulb.","params":{"v":[3,12],"b":[2,3]},"where":"v%b==0"}],"explain":{"kid":["A circuit is a loop. Tiny charges flow all the way round it. The battery gives each charge a push and some energy.","Current is how much charge goes past a point each second. We measure it in amps (A) with an ammeter. It sits in the loop.","Voltage is how much energy each charge drops off at a part, like a bulb. We measure it in volts (V) with a voltmeter. It sits across the part, on its own side loop.","A bulb does not use up current. The same current comes out as goes in. The bulb takes energy, not charge."],"adult":["Current is the rate of flow of charge: I = Q ÷ t, so 1 A means 1 coulomb per second. In a single loop it is the same at every point, which is why an ammeter can go anywhere in series.","Potential difference is energy transferred per unit charge: V = E ÷ Q, so 1 V means 1 joule per coulomb. Around a series loop the voltages across the parts add up to the battery's voltage.","Ammeters have almost no resistance and go in series; voltmeters have a very high resistance and go in parallel. In metal wires the charges that move are electrons, drifting the opposite way to the conventional current arrow."]},"familyTip":"Look on a phone charger or a battery: it gives the voltage (for example 5 V or 1.5 V). Look at a kettle plug label for the current (for example 13 A). Talk about which one is a push and which one is a flow.","keyIdea":"Current is how fast charge flows round a circuit, measured in amps with an ammeter in the loop. Voltage (potential difference) is the energy each charge gives up or gains, measured in volts with a voltmeter across a part.","visual":{"type":"circuit","caption":"Current only flows round a complete loop of conductors. Put each object in the gap: does the bulb light?","opts":{"items":[["Copper wire",1],["Steel paper clip",1],["Aluminium foil",1],["Pencil lead (graphite)",1],["Plastic straw",0],["Wooden lolly stick",0],["Glass marble",0],["Rubber band",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcirc","caption":"Flagship lab: the circuit bench. Move the meters, add cells and bulbs, and watch charge and energy flow.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_0DyF7bl4zh":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A {m} kg rock sits on a ledge {h} m above the ground (g = 10 N/kg). How much energy is in its gravitational store, in J?","answer":"m*10*h","hint":"E = m × g × h, with g = 10 N/kg on Earth.","explain":"E = {m} × 10 × {h} = {=m*10*h} J.","params":{"m":[2,8],"h":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"You lift a box to twice the height. What happens to the energy in its gravitational store?","choices":["It doubles","It stays the same","It goes up by 4 times"],"answer":0,"hint":"E = m × g × h. What happens to the answer if h doubles?","explain":"Energy is proportional to height, so twice the height gives twice the energy."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A {g} g apple is lifted 2 m (g = 10 N/kg). How much energy is stored, in J? Change grams to kilograms first.","answer":"g/1000*10*2","hint":"Divide the grams by 1000 to get kilograms, then use E = m × g × h.","explain":"{g} g = {=g/1000} kg, so E = {=g/1000} × 10 × 2 = {=g/1000*10*2} J.","params":{"g":[100,500]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A ball is dropped. Ignoring air resistance, where does the energy in its gravitational store go?","choices":["Into its kinetic store","It is used up","It stays in the gravitational store"],"answer":0,"hint":"The ball gets lower and faster. Which store gets bigger as it speeds up?","explain":"Energy is not used up. As the ball falls, the gravitational store empties into the kinetic store, so it speeds up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A crate is lifted from a 2 m shelf to a 5 m shelf. Which height do you use in E = mgh?","choices":["3 m","5 m","7 m"],"answer":0,"hint":"The store changes by the height gained, not the final height.","explain":"Height gained = 5 m − 2 m = 3 m. Using 5 m or adding to 7 m are the common slips."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A 4 kg crate and a 1 kg crate sit on the same shelf. How does the energy in their gravitational stores compare?","choices":["The 4 kg crate has 4 times as much","They have the same","The 1 kg crate has more"],"answer":0,"hint":"Energy is proportional to mass.","explain":"Same g and same height, but 4 times the mass, so 4 times the energy."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"On the Moon, g = 1.6 N/kg. A 5 kg box is lifted {h} m. How much energy is stored, in J?","answer":"5*1.6*h","hint":"E = m × g × h, with g = 1.6 instead of 10.","explain":"E = 5 × 1.6 × {h} = {=5*1.6*h} J. On Earth the same lift would store {=5*10*h} J.","params":{"h":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A 2 kg crate stores 60 J in its gravitational store on Earth (g = 10 N/kg). How high is it, in metres?","answer":3,"hint":"Rearrange E = m × g × h: h = E ÷ (m × g).","explain":"h = 60 ÷ (2 × 10) = 60 ÷ 20 = 3 m."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Why does a heavy bag lifted up the stairs store more energy than a light bag lifted the same height?","choices":["It has more mass, and E is proportional to m","It took longer to carry","The stairs are steeper for it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which letter in E = m × g × h is different for the two bags?","explain":"g and h are the same for both, so the bag with more mass stores more energy. Time and steepness do not appear in the equation."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A {m} kg box is lifted {h} m on Earth (g = 10 N/kg). How much energy goes into its gravitational store, in joules?","answer":"m*10*h","hint":"Use E = m × g × h. Multiply the mass by 10, then by the height.","explain":"E = {m} × 10 × {h} = {=m*10*h} J.","params":{"m":[1,6],"h":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A {m} kg bag is moved from a 1 m shelf up to a {h} m shelf (g = 10 N/kg). How much does its gravitational store increase, in J?","answer":"m*10*(h-1)","hint":"Find the height gained first: the new height minus 1 m. Then use E = m × g × h.","explain":"Height gained = {h} − 1 = {=h-1} m, so E = {m} × 10 × {=h-1} = {=m*10*(h-1)} J.","params":{"m":[1,5],"h":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The same 2 kg crate is lifted 3 m on Earth and on the Moon. Why does it store less energy on the Moon?","choices":["g is smaller on the Moon","Its mass is smaller on the Moon","Energy cannot be stored on the Moon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Mass is the amount of matter. Does that change when you fly to the Moon?","explain":"The mass stays 2 kg. The gravitational field strength drops from 10 to 1.6 N/kg, so m × g × h is smaller."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Two people take the same box to the same shelf. One uses a long ramp, one lifts it straight up. Ignoring friction, which box stores more energy?","choices":["They store the same","The one pushed up the ramp","The one lifted straight up"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the lab, every route to the 3 m shelf gave the same bar.","explain":"Only the mass, g and the height gained matter. Both boxes gained the same height, so they store the same energy."}],"explain":{"kid":["When you lift a bag onto a shelf, you do work against gravity. That energy is not lost. It goes into the gravitational store of the bag and the Earth.","The amount depends on three things. A heavier bag stores more. A higher shelf stores more. And stronger gravity stores more. So E = m × g × h. On Earth, g is about 10 N/kg.","Only the change in height counts. Lift a box from a 1 m shelf to a 3 m shelf and the height gained is 2 m. The route does not matter. A ramp and a straight lift to the same shelf store the same energy.","On the Moon, g is only 1.6 N/kg. The same lift stores much less energy. When something falls, its gravitational store empties into its kinetic store, so it speeds up. Ignoring air, all of it ends up as kinetic energy at the ground."],"adult":["This is Key Stage 3 physics in England. The energy topic asks pupils to treat energy as a quantity that can be calculated and to describe changes in a system, including changes in position in a field. The forces topic gives weight = mass × gravitational field strength, with g = 10 N/kg on Earth and different values on other planets. GCSE adds the equation Ep = m g h, often with g = 9.8 N/kg. In the US, NGSS MS-PS3-2 asks students to model how potential energy changes when objects interacting at a distance change their arrangement.","Two ideas carry most of the reasoning. First, the energy is proportional to each of m, g and h, so doubling one doubles the store. Second, only the change in vertical height matters. Ignoring friction, the path taken does not change the energy stored. The lab uses g = 10 N/kg on Earth, 3.7 N/kg on Mars and 1.6 N/kg on the Moon.","Common mistakes: using the final height instead of the height gained, or adding the two heights. Leaving g out and multiplying only mass by height. Using grams or centimetres without converting to kilograms and metres. Thinking a longer ramp stores more energy. And saying the energy is used up when an object falls, instead of transferred to the kinetic store. Be careful with the Moon too: the mass is the same there, but g is smaller, so the energy is smaller.","In the lab students lift a crate up a 5 m scale while a bar shows the gravitational store, swap 1, 2 and 4 kg crates at the same height, and send a crate to the same shelf by a straight lift, stairs and a ramp. They repeat a lift on Earth, Mars and the Moon, then let the crate fall and watch the gravitational store drain into the kinetic store."]},"familyTip":"Carry a bag of shopping up the stairs together and ask where the energy went. Then ask how you could get it back (drop or roll something down). Try the numbers: a 2 kg bag lifted 3 m stores 2 × 10 × 3 = 60 J.","keyIdea":"Lifting an object stores energy in its gravitational store. The energy stored is E = m × g × h: mass (kg) times gravitational field strength (N/kg) times the height gained (m). Double the mass or the height and the energy doubles. Weaker gravity, as on the Moon, stores less. When the object falls, that energy moves to the kinetic store.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"More energy in the gravitational store, or less? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["⬆️ More energy stored","⬇️ Less energy stored"],"items":[["Lift the same crate twice as high",0],["Do the same lift on the Moon",1],["Use a crate with half the mass",1],["Lift a heavier crate to the same shelf",0],["Lower the crate to a shelf nearer the floor",1],["Do the same lift where g is stronger",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaglift","caption":"Flagship lab: lift a crate and watch E = mgh, change the mass, try three routes, lift on Earth, Mars and the Moon, then let it fall.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_s9Whgzsav0":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which has the most kinetic energy?","choices":["A 2 kg ball at 1 m/s","A 1 kg ball at 3 m/s","A 4 kg ball at 1 m/s","A 10 kg ball standing still"],"answer":1,"hint":"Mass counts once. Speed counts twice. Something still has none.","explain":"The 1 kg ball at 3 m/s has ½ × 1 × 9 = 4.5 J. The 4 kg ball at 1 m/s has 2 J, the 2 kg ball 1 J, and the still ball 0 J."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A car speeds up from 30 mph to 60 mph. Compared with before, its kinetic energy is…","choices":["the same","2 times as big","4 times as big","60 times as big"],"answer":2,"hint":"Twice the speed. Square the 2.","explain":"Twice the speed means 2 × 2 = 4 times the kinetic energy. That is why crashes at high speed do so much more damage."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A parked lorry has a mass of 40 000 kg. What is its kinetic energy?","choices":["Zero","40 000 J","Very large, because it is heavy","Half its mass"],"answer":0,"hint":"Kinetic energy is energy of movement.","explain":"It is not moving, so it has no kinetic energy, however heavy it is."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Sam says, 'Doubling speed doubles kinetic energy, just like mass.' What is wrong?","choices":["Nothing, Sam is right","Speed is squared, so doubling speed gives four times the energy","Speed does not change kinetic energy","Doubling mass gives four times the energy"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at 1 m/s and 2 m/s for the same cart: 1 J and 4 J.","explain":"Mass counts once, so it doubles the energy. Speed is squared, so doubling it gives four times the energy."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A car going at 10 m/s and a lorry with 10 times its mass going at 3 m/s. Which has more kinetic energy?","choices":["The car","The lorry","They are the same","You cannot tell from mass and speed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the multipliers: mass ×10 for the lorry, speed squared for the car.","explain":"Compare mass × speed². Car: 1 × 10 × 10 = 100. Lorry: 10 × 3 × 3 = 90. The car has slightly more, even though the lorry is 10 times heavier."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Why are 20 mph zones used near schools instead of 30 mph?","choices":["Cars are lighter at 20 mph","At 20 mph a car has under half the kinetic energy it has at 30 mph, so crashes do far less harm","Kinetic energy does not depend on speed","Slower cars have more energy"],"answer":1,"hint":"Compare 2 × 2 with 3 × 3.","explain":"Energy goes with speed squared: 20² is 400 and 30² is 900, so 20 mph has less than half the crash energy."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A ball has {e} J of kinetic energy. Its speed is doubled. How many joules does it have now?","answer":"4*e","hint":"Double the speed means four times the energy.","explain":"2 × 2 = 4, so {e} J becomes {=4*e} J.","params":{"e":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A trolley's mass is multiplied by {k}. Its speed stays the same. How many times bigger is its kinetic energy?","answer":"k","hint":"Mass and kinetic energy go up in step.","explain":"Mass counts once, so {k} times the mass gives {k} times the energy.","params":{"k":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"A runner's speed is multiplied by {k}. How many times bigger is her kinetic energy?","answer":"k*k","hint":"Multiply the speed change by itself.","explain":"{k} × {k} = {=k*k}, so her kinetic energy is {=k*k} times bigger.","params":{"k":[2,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A shopping trolley moves at 1 m/s. You fill it so its mass doubles, and push it at the same speed. Its kinetic energy…","choices":["stays the same","doubles","goes up four times","halves"],"answer":1,"hint":"Mass and kinetic energy go up in step.","explain":"Twice the mass at the same speed gives twice the kinetic energy. Only speed is squared."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A cyclist goes {k} times faster than before. Her mass stays the same. How many times bigger is her kinetic energy?","answer":"k*k","hint":"Speed is squared: multiply the change by itself.","explain":"{k} times the speed gives {k} × {k} = {=k*k} times the kinetic energy.","params":{"k":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A table shows a cart's kinetic energy at 1, 2 and 3 m/s: 1 J, 4 J and 9 J. What will the graph of energy against speed look like?","choices":["A straight line through the points","A curve that gets steeper as speed goes up","A flat line","A curve that levels off"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at how much the energy goes up for each extra 1 m/s.","explain":"The jumps are 3 J, then 5 J. Each extra 1 m/s adds more energy than the last, so the curve gets steeper."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A cart has {e} J of kinetic energy. You load it to {k} times its mass and keep the same speed. How many joules does it have now?","answer":"e*k","hint":"Mass multiplies the energy once.","explain":"{k} times the mass gives {k} times the energy: {e} × {k} = {=e*k} J.","params":{"e":[2,9],"k":[2,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Kinetic energy is the energy something has because it is moving. A still object has none. The faster it goes, or the heavier it is, the more it has. You can see this in a crash: more energy means a bigger dent.","Mass and energy go up in step. Put twice as much in a trolley, push it at the same speed, and it has twice the energy. Four times the mass gives four times the energy.","Speed counts more. Twice the speed gives four times the energy, because 2 × 2 = 4. Three times the speed gives nine times the energy. So on a graph of energy against speed, the line bends upward and gets steeper.","This is why speed limits matter. A car at 60 mph has four times the energy of the same car at 30 mph. In a crash, all that energy goes into bending metal and hurting people."],"adult":["This lesson meets NGSS MS-PS3-1: learners construct and interpret data to describe how kinetic energy depends on mass and on speed. The formula is kinetic energy = ½ × mass × speed², but at this stage the aim is the shape of the relationship (proportional to mass, proportional to speed squared), read from tables, graphs and a cart model rather than lengthy calculation.","The most common misconception is that doubling the speed doubles the energy. Learners treat speed like mass. Ask them to compare 1 m/s and 2 m/s for the same cart in the data, where the energy goes from 1 J to 4 J. A second slip is to think a heavy object always has more energy, even when it is moving slowly; the car against lorry and football against cricket ball comparisons show speed can win.","Be careful with graphs: some learners draw a straight line through the first two points and extrapolate. The table step asks them to predict 3 m/s and 4 m/s before the curve appears. Also make sure they know that a parked object has zero kinetic energy, however heavy it is, and that energy is measured in joules (J).","For road safety, connect the maths to real decisions: going from 20 to 30 mph raises crash energy about 2.25 times, which is why 20 mph zones near schools reduce serious injuries. Ask your learner to estimate how much more energy a car carries at 40 mph than at 20 mph."]},"familyTip":"Roll a toy car down a ramp from low and then higher up, so it goes faster, into a pile of paper cups. Then tape a coin or two on top and roll it from the low spot again. Which made the bigger mess: more mass, or more speed?","keyIdea":"Kinetic energy is the energy an object has because it is moving. It increases in proportion to mass: twice the mass at the same speed means twice the kinetic energy. It increases with the square of speed: twice the speed means four times the kinetic energy, and three times the speed means nine times. So a graph of kinetic energy against speed curves upward, and small increases in speed cause much bigger increases in crash energy.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What happens to the kinetic energy? Drag each change to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Doubles","Four times as big","No change"],"items":[["Double the mass, same speed",0],["Double the speed, same mass",1],["Paint it a new colour",2],["Four times the mass, same speed",1],["Halve the mass, double the speed",0],["Turn round, same speed",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagkemv","caption":"Flagship lab: load a cart with mass, change its speed, fill a data table, compare moving objects and crash-test a car.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_d-EKO-pKkP":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A tray holds {a} coins, {b} wooden sticks and {d} rubber bands. How many objects are conductors?","answer":"a","hint":"Only one of these materials is a metal.","explain":"Coins are metal, so they conduct. Wood and rubber are insulators. That makes {a} conductors.","params":{"a":[2,7],"b":[1,5],"d":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A class tests {n} objects. {k} keep the bulb off. How many are conductors?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Objects that keep the bulb off are insulators. The rest are conductors.","explain":"{n} take away {k} insulators leaves {=n-k} conductors.","params":{"n":[9,14],"k":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which of these is an insulator?","choices":["a plastic ruler","an iron nail","aluminium foil"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the one that is not a metal.","explain":"Plastic blocks electricity, so a plastic ruler is an insulator. The nail and the foil are metals and conduct."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Zoe says a shiny plastic bead will light the bulb because it is shiny. Is she right?","choices":["No, it is plastic, so it is an insulator","Yes, shiny things conduct","Yes, but only a little"],"answer":0,"hint":"What is the bead made of?","explain":"Shiny is not the clue. The bead is plastic, which is an insulator, so the bulb stays off."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A clip touches only the plastic coat of a wire. What happens?","choices":["The bulb stays off","The bulb lights","The battery gets stronger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part of the wire is the conductor: the coat or the core?","explain":"Only the copper core conducts. The plastic coat is an insulator, so no electricity flows and the bulb stays off."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Most good conductors of electricity are…","choices":["metals","heavy things","hard things"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the paper clip, the coin and the foil. What do they have in common?","explain":"Metals are good conductors. Heavy or hard things, such as a stone or a wooden block, can still be insulators."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A wooden stick keeps the bulb off. Sam says the battery is flat. How can he check?","choices":["Put a metal key in the gap","Shake the bulb","Use a longer stick"],"answer":0,"hint":"Test with something you already know is a conductor.","explain":"If a metal key lights the bulb, the battery works. That shows the wood is an insulator."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why are cables made of copper inside and plastic outside?","choices":["Copper carries the electricity and plastic keeps it off our hands","Plastic carries the electricity and copper makes it strong","Both help electricity flow faster"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is the conductor and which is the insulator?","explain":"Copper is a metal conductor, so it carries the current. Plastic is an insulator, so it keeps people safe."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A steel spoon goes in the gap and the bulb lights. What is steel?","choices":["A conductor","An insulator","A battery"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did electricity get through the spoon to the bulb?","explain":"The bulb lit, so electricity flowed through the steel. Steel is a metal and a conductor."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Ravi tests {n} objects. {k} of them light the bulb. How many objects are insulators?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Every object that did not light the bulb is an insulator. Take the conductors away from the total.","explain":"{n} objects take away {k} conductors leaves {=n-k} insulators.","params":{"n":[8,12],"k":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A box has {m} metal objects and {p} plastic objects. How many will light the bulb?","answer":"m","hint":"Which of the two materials is a conductor?","explain":"Metals conduct and plastic does not, so the {m} metal objects light the bulb.","params":{"m":[2,9],"p":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Why is the handle of an electrician's screwdriver made of rubber or plastic?","choices":["They are insulators, so electricity cannot reach the hand","They are conductors, so electricity flows better","They are shiny"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which kind of material blocks electricity?","explain":"Rubber and plastic are insulators. They stop electricity flowing into the person holding the tool."}],"explain":{"kid":["A bulb lights only when electricity can flow all the way round a loop, from the battery, through the bulb and back. Leave a gap and the flow stops. Put an object in the gap and you can test it.","If the bulb lights, the object is a conductor. It lets electricity through. If the bulb stays off, the object is an insulator. It blocks the flow.","Metals are good conductors. A steel paper clip, a coin, foil and an iron nail all light the bulb. Plastic, rubber and wood do not. A shiny plastic bead stays off, so shiny is not the clue. Ask: is it metal?","A wire has a copper core inside a plastic coat. The copper carries the electricity. The plastic is an insulator, so it keeps the electricity off your hands. Only test things with a small battery, never with a plug socket."],"adult":["This is a Year 4 topic in the national curriculum for England. Pupils should recognise some common conductors and insulators. They should also link metals with being good conductors. It builds on making a simple series circuit, where a bulb lights only in a complete loop. The same unit asks pupils to set up simple fair tests.","Metals conduct because some of their electrons are free to move. In plastic, rubber, dry wood and glass the electrons are held in place, so almost no current flows. The test circuit is a fair test: only the object in the gap changes. If a metal key lights the bulb, the cell and bulb work. So an object that leaves it dark is an insulator.","Common mistakes: thinking shiny means conductor, so a shiny plastic bead should work. Thinking hard or heavy things conduct. Thinking a wire conducts wherever you touch it, even on its plastic coat. Thinking the coat helps the current flow. Blaming a flat battery when the gap holds an insulator. Pencil lead (graphite) is a non-metal that conducts a little, so the bulb may glow dimly. Save it as an extra once the metal rule is secure.","In the lab learners close a gap with a steel key and test six everyday objects. They test a shiny plastic bead against a dull iron nail. They clip onto a wire's plastic coat, then onto its stripped copper end. Last, they pick conductors and insulators for a cable, a plug and a screwdriver."]},"familyTip":"With a 1.5 V battery, a torch bulb and two wires with clips, test things from a drawer: a spoon, a key, a pencil, a lolly stick, foil, an eraser. Predict first, then test. Never test anything with a plug socket, which can kill.","keyIdea":"An electrical conductor is a material that lets electricity flow through it, so it completes a circuit and the bulb lights. An electrical insulator blocks the flow, so the bulb stays off. Metals such as copper, steel, iron and aluminium are good conductors; plastic, rubber, wood and glass are insulators. Shiny, hard or heavy is not the clue: test the material. Wires have a metal core to carry the electricity and a plastic coat to keep it off your hands.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Conductor or insulator? Drag each object, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Conductor: lets electricity through","Insulator: blocks electricity"],"items":[["steel paper clip",0],["plastic spoon",1],["aluminium foil",0],["rubber band",1],["iron nail",0],["wooden lolly stick",1],["copper wire",0],["glass marble",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcond","caption":"Flagship lab: test objects in the gap of a circuit, sort conductors from insulators, see why shiny is not the clue and why wires have a coat.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_nsZhXdxV14":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which is stronger, so it can hurt you badly?","choices":["A wall socket","A torch battery","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the two bars in the lab. Which one was huge?","explain":"A wall socket gives over 100 volts. A torch battery gives about 1.5 volts, so it is safe to hold."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How do you unplug a lamp the safe way?","choices":["Switch off, then pull the plug","Pull hard on the cable","Pull it out while it is on"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part do you hold, and what do you do first?","explain":"Switch off at the wall, then hold the plug and pull. Pulling the cable can break the wires inside."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Your toast is stuck in the toaster. What do you do?","choices":["Ask a grown-up to unplug it","Get it out with a fork","Shake it while it is on"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is metal a safe thing to put inside a toaster?","explain":"A metal fork can carry electricity to your hand. A grown-up must switch off and unplug the toaster first."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Your kite is stuck in a power line. What do you do?","choices":["Let go and tell a grown-up","Climb up to get it","Pull the string hard"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can the string carry electricity down to you?","explain":"Let go and tell a grown-up. The string can carry electricity, and you must never climb a pole or pylon."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A remote needs 2 batteries. A toy car needs {k} batteries. How many batteries for both?","answer":"2+k","hint":"Start at 2 and count on {k} more.","explain":"2 batteries for the remote and {k} for the car make {=2+k} batteries.","params":{"k":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You see a cable with the wires showing. What do you do?","choices":["Do not touch it and tell a grown-up","Wrap it in paper","Plug it in to see if it works"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is a broken cable safe to touch?","explain":"Bare wires can give you an electric shock. Do not touch it, and tell a grown-up so they can deal with it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"There are {t} sockets in a room. {u} have a plug in. How many are empty?","answer":"t-u","hint":"Start at {t} and take away the {u} with plugs.","explain":"{t} take away {u} leaves {=t-u} empty sockets.","params":{"t":[4,8],"u":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why should a hairdryer be kept away from the bath and the sink?","choices":["Water can carry electricity to you","It makes the water too hot","It will dry up the water"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about water and electricity together.","explain":"Water and electricity do not mix. Water can carry the electricity to you and give you a shock."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Lots of plugs are pushed into one socket. Why is that a problem?","choices":["It can get hot and start a fire","The lights will get brighter","Nothing, it is fine"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happened to the crowded socket in the lab?","explain":"Too many plugs in one socket can overheat and start a fire. Ask a grown-up to unplug some."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which one gets its electricity from a socket in the wall?","choices":["A toaster","A torch","A remote"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the one with a cable and a plug.","explain":"A toaster has a cable and a plug, so it uses mains electricity. A torch and a remote use batteries."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A torch needs 2 batteries. You have {n} torches. How many batteries do you need?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Count in 2s, once for each torch.","explain":"Each torch needs 2 batteries, so {n} torches need {=2*n} batteries.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"In the kitchen, {a} things plug in and {b} things use batteries. How many things is that in all?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Start at {a} and count on {b} more.","explain":"{a} plug-in things and {b} battery things make {=a+b} things.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Your hands are wet from washing. You want to turn on the light. What do you do?","choices":["Dry my hands first","Be very quick","Use one wet finger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does being quick stop the water carrying electricity?","explain":"Water can carry electricity to you, so dry your hands first. Being quick does not help."}],"explain":{"kid":["A lamp has a cable and a plug. It gets electricity from the wall. A torch has batteries inside.","Electricity from the wall is very strong. It can hurt you badly. A small battery is weak and safe to hold.","Water and electricity do not mix. Dry your hands before you touch a plug or a switch.","Never poke a fork, a finger or a toy into a socket or a toaster. Tell a grown-up if a cable is broken. Fly your kite in an open park, far from power lines."],"adult":["In England, the Year 4 science unit on electricity asks children to name everyday appliances that run on electricity. Its guidance adds that pupils should be taught to work safely with electricity. This lesson brings that forward for 6 and 7 year olds, who already use switches, sockets and battery toys. The words to use are mains electricity, socket, plug, cable, switch, appliance, battery and electric shock.","The science behind the rules: mains electricity in the UK is about 230 volts (about 120 volts in the US). An AA battery is 1.5 volts. Tap water and wet skin let electricity into the body far more easily than dry skin. That is why UK bathrooms use pull-cord switches and have no ordinary sockets near the bath. Metal is a good conductor, so a fork in a plugged-in toaster can carry electricity straight to a hand. Power lines carry thousands of volts, and the electricity can jump a gap. A kite string does not have to touch the line to be dangerous.","Common mistakes to watch for: thinking a battery toy and a wall socket are the same, or that wet hands are fine if you are quick. Pulling a plug out by its cable strains the wires inside until they break. Too many adaptors in one socket can overheat and start a fire. Children also need to hear that small batteries are safe to hold but must never go in the mouth. A swallowed button battery can burn the throat within hours.","In the lab your child sorts six things into plug-in or battery, tests how strong a battery and a wall socket are, dries wet hands and moves a hairdryer away from the sink, spots three dangers in a kitchen, switches off and pulls a plug out the safe way, and drags a kite away from power lines. Every step works with big buttons as well as taps and drags. Read the Challenge aloud together."]},"familyTip":"Walk round your home together and find three things that plug in and three that use batteries. Then show your child where the switch for each socket is, how to switch off before unplugging, and which grown-up to tell if a cable looks damaged.","keyIdea":"Some things plug into a socket in the wall. That is mains electricity, and it is strong enough to hurt you badly. Other things, like a torch or a remote, use small batteries, which are much weaker. Keep water away from plugs and switches, and dry wet hands first. Never put metal or fingers into a socket or a toaster. To unplug, switch off and pull the plug, not the cable. Tell a grown-up about a broken cable or too many plugs in one socket. Fly kites far away from power lines.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Safe or not safe? Drag each one, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Safe","Not safe"],"items":[["Dry hands on a switch",0],["Holding a torch",0],["Pulling out the plug",0],["A fork in a toaster",1],["Wet hands on a plug",1],["A kite by power lines",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagzap","caption":"Flagship lab: plug or battery, battery against socket, water, kitchen dangers, unplugging safely and kites near power lines.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_iGSfQg3g5c":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does sunlight do to the ground?","choices":["it warms it","it cools it","nothing: it only gives light"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of how a path feels after the sun shines on it all day.","explain":"Sunlight gives warmth as well as light, so the ground it shines on warms up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why is it cooler under a tree on a sunny day?","choices":["the leaves block the sunlight","the tree makes cold air","trees are wet"],"answer":0,"hint":"What stops the sunlight from reaching the ground there?","explain":"The leaves block the sunlight and make shade. The tree does not make cold; it just stops the sun warming that spot."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Sand and water sit in the sun all morning. Which feels hotter?","choices":["the sand","the water","both the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the beach: the sand and the sea on a sunny day.","explain":"The sand warms up fast and feels hot. Water warms up slowly, so it feels cooler."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"When is a sunny slide hottest?","choices":["in the middle of the day","early in the morning","at night"],"answer":0,"hint":"When is the sun high in the sky?","explain":"In the middle of the day the sun is high and has shone on the slide for hours, so it is hottest."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which roof keeps a dog's water bowl cool in the sun?","choices":["an umbrella","clear plastic","a net with big holes"],"answer":0,"hint":"The roof needs to stop the sunlight, not let it through.","explain":"An umbrella blocks the sunlight and makes shade. Clear plastic and a net let lots of sunlight through."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You want to test if colour matters in the sun. Where should the dark can and the white can go?","choices":["both in the sun","one in the sun and one in the shade","both in the shade"],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep everything the same except the colour.","explain":"Put both in the sun so only the colour is different. Then the dark can warms more, and you know colour did it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"{a} children sit in the sun and {b} sit under a big umbrella. How many children are in the shade?","answer":"b","hint":"The umbrella blocks the sunlight. Who is under it?","explain":"The {b} children under the umbrella are in the shade. The {a} children in the sun will feel warmer.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A path has 10 stones. A tree shades {a} of them. How many stones are in the sun?","answer":"10-a","hint":"Take the shaded stones away from all 10.","explain":"10 − {a} = {=10-a}, so {=10-a} stones are in the sun and get warmer.","params":{"a":[3,7]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Two toy cars sit outside. One is in the sun and one is in the shade. Which one gets warmer?","choices":["the one in the sun","the one in the shade","both the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about which car the sunlight shines on.","explain":"Sunlight shines on the car in the sun and warms it. The shade blocks the sunlight, so that car stays cooler."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A sandpit has 10 squares. An umbrella shades {a} of them. How many squares are still in the sun?","answer":"10-a","hint":"Start with all 10 squares and take away the shaded ones.","explain":"10 − {a} = {=10-a}, so {=10-a} squares are still in the sun and will warm up more.","params":{"a":[2,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"There are {a} stones in the sun and {b} stones in the shade. How many stones will get hot?","answer":"a","hint":"Only the things the sunlight shines on get hot.","explain":"Only the {a} stones in the sun get hot. The {b} stones in the shade stay cooler, because the sunlight is blocked.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which T-shirt gets hotter in the sun?","choices":["the black one","the white one","both the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Dark colours take in more sunlight than light colours.","explain":"The black T-shirt takes in more of the sunlight, so it gets hotter. The white one sends more of the light away."}],"explain":{"kid":["The sun gives us light. It gives us warmth too. When sunlight shines on the ground, the ground gets warm.","Shade is a place where the sunlight is blocked. A tree, a roof or an umbrella can make shade. A stone in the sun gets hot. A stone in the shade stays cool. Shade does not make things cold. It just stops the sun.","Some things warm up faster. A dark T-shirt gets hotter than a white one. Sand gets hot fast, but water warms up slowly. That is why the sand can burn your feet while the sea feels cool.","In the morning the sun is low, and the slide is cool. In the middle of the day the sun is high, and the slide can be hot. A big umbrella can keep a spot cool."],"adult":["This matches two US kindergarten standards. K-PS3-1 asks children to 'make observations to determine the effect of sunlight on Earth's surface'. K-PS3-2 asks them to 'use tools and materials to design and build a structure that will reduce the warming effect of sunlight on an area'. At this age children compare warmer and cooler. They do not need to read degrees.","The science: sunlight carries energy, and a surface that takes it in warms up. Shade is where something blocks the direct sunlight, so less energy lands there. Warm air still warms shaded things a little. Dark surfaces take in more light than pale ones. Water warms more slowly than sand, as it needs more energy to heat and it mixes the heat down through its depth.","Common mistakes: thinking shade or a tree 'makes cold' instead of blocking sunlight, thinking the sun only gives light, and expecting a clear roof to keep things cool. Children also compare two things that differ in more than one way. The ground is often hottest in the early afternoon, a little after the sun is highest, as it keeps soaking up sunlight.","In the lab your child puts one stone in the sun and one in the shade and waits until midday. Next they compare a dark and a white can and feel sand and water. Then they drag the sun across the sky to warm and cool a slide, and test roofs until one keeps a dog's water bowl cool."]},"familyTip":"On a sunny day, put two of the same toy outside, one in the sun and one in the shade. Feel them after lunch. Touch a dark car and a white car, or the sand and the sea, and talk about which is hotter and why. Then build a shade for a toy with a towel.","keyIdea":"Sunlight warms the ground, the water and the things it shines on. A thing in the sun warms up more than the same thing in the shade, because shade is just a place where something blocks the sunlight. Dark things and sand warm up faster than white things and water, the ground is warmest in the middle of the day, and a roof that blocks the sun keeps a place cooler.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it get warm in the sun, or does it make shade? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Gets warm in the sun","Blocks the sun to make shade"],"items":[["sand",0],["a dark car",0],["a metal slide",0],["a rock",0],["a tree",1],["an umbrella",1],["a sun hat",1],["a roof",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsun","caption":"Flagship lab: compare sun and shade, dark and light, sand and water, follow the sun across the sky and build a roof that keeps water cool.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_NzCNuABT3E":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What must a circuit have for current to flow?","choices":["a complete loop with no gaps","one wire from the cell to the bulb","a switch that is open"],"answer":0,"hint":"Trace the path from one end of the cell back to the other end.","explain":"Current only flows round a complete circuit: a full loop from one end of the cell, through the parts, back to the other end."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which component changes electrical energy into sound?","choices":["a buzzer","a motor","a cell"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a door bell or an alarm.","explain":"A buzzer changes electrical energy into sound. A motor gives movement, and a cell gives the push."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You add a second bulb in series with the first. What happens?","choices":["both bulbs are dimmer","both bulbs are brighter","nothing changes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Two bulbs in one loop give more resistance.","explain":"In series the two bulbs share one path. More resistance means less current, so both are dimmer."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"In a house, one light can be off while the others are on. How are the lights wired?","choices":["in parallel","in series","with no switches"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which kind of circuit lets one bulb go out while the rest stay on?","explain":"They are in parallel: each light has its own branch and switch, so one can be off while the others stay on."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why are wires made of copper with a plastic coat?","choices":["copper conducts and plastic insulates","plastic conducts and copper insulates","both block the current"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is a metal, and what do metals do to current?","explain":"Copper is a metal, so it is a good conductor and carries the current. Plastic is an insulator, so it keeps the current off your hands."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What does the voltage of a cell tell you?","choices":["how big a push it gives","how long its wires are","how bright the room is"],"answer":0,"hint":"The cell pushes the current round the loop.","explain":"Voltage is the size of the push the cell gives. A bigger voltage pushes more current through the same bulb, so it glows brighter."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Fairy lights have {a} bulbs in parallel. {b} bulbs are broken. How many still shine?","answer":"a-b","where":"b<a","hint":"In parallel a broken bulb only stops its own branch.","explain":"Each bulb has its own branch, so only the {b} broken ones are dark. {a} − {b} = {=a-b} still shine.","params":{"a":[8,20],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A battery is made of {a} cells joined in a row. How many cells do {b} of these batteries have?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Each battery has {a} cells. Count them for every battery.","explain":"{b} batteries × {a} cells = {=a*b} cells in all.","params":{"a":[2,4],"b":[2,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which word means two or more cells joined together?","choices":["a battery","a bulb","a switch"],"answer":0,"hint":"One cell gives a push. Join cells and you get a bigger push.","explain":"A battery is two or more cells joined together. One single unit on its own is a cell."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A parallel circuit has {a} bulbs, each on its own branch. You unscrew {b} of them. How many bulbs stay lit?","answer":"a-b","where":"b<a","hint":"In parallel each bulb has its own complete branch. Only the unscrewed ones go out.","explain":"Each branch is its own loop, so only the {b} unscrewed bulbs go out. {a} − {b} = {=a-b} bulbs stay lit.","params":{"a":[3,6],"b":[1,2]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A series circuit has {a} bulbs in one loop. One of them is unscrewed. How many bulbs stay lit?","answer":"0","hint":"In series there is only one path. What does one gap do to that path?","explain":"Unscrewing one bulb makes a gap in the only path, so the current stops and all {a} bulbs go out. 0 stay lit.","params":{"a":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A circuit has a cell, a bulb and an open switch. What does the bulb do?","choices":["it stays off","it lights","it flickers"],"answer":0,"hint":"An open switch makes a gap. Can the current get round?","explain":"An open switch makes a gap, so the circuit is not complete. No current flows and the bulb stays off."}],"explain":{"kid":["A circuit is a loop that electricity flows round. Each part of it is a component. A cell gives the push. Two or more cells joined make a battery. A bulb gives light, a buzzer makes a sound and a motor spins. Wires join them up.","The flow is called current. The push from the cell is the voltage. Current only flows round a complete circuit. That means a full loop with no gaps, from one end of the cell, through the parts, and back to the other end. Wires are made of metal because metals are conductors. Plastic is an insulator, so it blocks the flow.","A switch is a gap you can control. When it is closed, the circuit is complete and the bulb lights. When it is open, there is a gap, so the bulb goes off. Open does not let the electricity out.","In a series circuit all the parts are in one loop. Add a second bulb and both get dimmer, because two bulbs give more resistance and less current flows. Take one bulb out and they all go off. In a parallel circuit each bulb has its own branch. Each one is as bright as one bulb alone, and if one goes out, the rest stay on."],"adult":["This lesson matches the English national curriculum for Year 4 Electricity: children should 'construct a simple series electrical circuit, identifying and naming its basic parts, including cells, wires, bulbs, switches and buzzers', and 'recognise that a switch opens and closes a circuit'. It also adds the Year 6 idea that brightness depends on the number of cells and bulbs, and the words series and parallel.","The key terms are component, cell, battery, current, voltage, resistance, conductor, insulator, switch, complete circuit, series and parallel. In everyday speech a single AA cell is called a battery. In science a battery is two or more cells joined, and the cell is the single unit.","Common mistakes: thinking one wire from the cell is enough, thinking an open switch lets the current through, and thinking the bulb uses up the current. Another slip is to believe that a second bulb in series gets the same current as one bulb alone. In fact both bulbs are dimmer. In parallel each branch gets the full voltage of the cell, so each bulb is as bright as one alone, but the cell gives more current in total and runs down faster.","In the lab your learner finds six components by name, closes two gaps to make a complete circuit and works a switch. Then they add a second bulb in series and in parallel and unscrew one, to see which bulbs stay on. Please be careful at home: mains electricity is never used for experiments, only small cells."]},"familyTip":"Look at a set of fairy lights or a torch together. Ask: is there a switch? Where does it make a gap? If one fairy light goes out and the rest stay on, are the bulbs in series or in parallel? Count the cells in a torch or a remote and talk about why more cells give a bigger push.","keyIdea":"Each part of a circuit is a component. A cell gives the push, called voltage; a battery is two or more cells joined. Current is the flow of electricity, and it only flows round a complete circuit, a loop of conductors with no gaps. A switch opens and closes a gap. In a series circuit everything sits in one loop, so extra bulbs add resistance, every bulb is dimmer and one gap stops them all. In a parallel circuit each bulb has its own branch, so each is as bright as one bulb alone and the others stay on if one is removed.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Series or parallel? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Series: one loop","Parallel: a branch each"],"items":[["one gap stops every bulb",0],["adding a bulb dims them all",0],["only one path for the current",0],["each bulb has its own branch",1],["one bulb out, the rest stay on",1],["each bulb gets the full voltage",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagseries","caption":"Flagship lab: find six components, close the gaps to complete a circuit, work a switch, then add a second bulb in series and in parallel and unscrew one.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_akBotspaf2":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which of these stores chemical energy?","choices":["a banana","a ringing bell","a beam of sunlight"],"answer":0,"hint":"Food and batteries store this kind of energy.","explain":"Food stores chemical energy, so a banana does. A ringing bell gives out sound, and sunlight is light energy on the move."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A book sits on a high shelf. Does it have energy?","choices":["yes, gravitational energy","no, it is not moving","only when someone reads it"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens if it gets knocked off the shelf?","explain":"Lifting the book stored gravitational energy. If it falls, that stored energy turns into kinetic energy, then sound and heat when it lands."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"When a phone battery goes flat, its energy has…","choices":["changed into light, sound and heat that spread out","been destroyed","gone back into the charger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can energy ever be destroyed?","explain":"Energy is never destroyed. The phone changed it into light, sound and heat, which spread out into the room."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A kettle gets {=a+b} units of electrical energy. {a} units heat the water. The rest warm the kitchen air. How many units warm the air?","answer":"b","hint":"All the energy that goes in must come out somewhere.","explain":"{=a+b} − {a} = {b} units warm the air. The energy out adds up to the {=a+b} units that went in.","params":{"a":[7,9],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which word means the energy of moving things?","choices":["kinetic","chemical","elastic"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is the energy a rolling ball or a running dog has.","explain":"Kinetic energy is the energy of moving things. Chemical and elastic energy are stored."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A lamp gives out {a} units of light and {b} units of heat. How many units of electrical energy went in?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Energy in equals all the energy out added up.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} units went in. The lamp changed every unit into light or heat.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[5,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"You pull a catapult band further back. What happens?","choices":["it stores more elastic energy and the stone flies further","it stores less energy","nothing changes"],"answer":0,"hint":"More stretch, more of which energy?","explain":"More stretch stores more elastic energy. That becomes more kinetic energy, so the stone flies faster and further."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which of these is energy on the move?","choices":["sound from a guitar","a new battery","a stretched spring"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for energy that travels from place to place.","explain":"Sound travels from the guitar to your ears. A battery and a stretched spring store energy instead."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which energy is stored in a stretched rubber band?","choices":["elastic energy","sound energy","light energy"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is not moving yet. What happens when you let go?","explain":"A stretched band stores elastic energy. When you let go, it turns into kinetic energy and the band flies."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A battery has {=a+b} energy blocks. A torch turns {a} of them into heat. The rest become light. How many become light?","answer":"b","hint":"No block can vanish. Take the heat blocks away from all the blocks.","explain":"{=a+b} − {a} = {b} blocks become light. Every block goes somewhere: none are used up.","params":{"a":[6,9],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What does a torch do with energy?","choices":["changes chemical energy into light and heat","makes new light energy from nothing","turns light into chemical energy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where does the energy start, before the switch is on?","explain":"The battery stores chemical energy. It flows as electrical energy and the bulb gives it out as light and heat. Nothing is made from nothing."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A speaker gets {=a+b} units of electrical energy. {a} units come out as sound. The rest come out as heat. How many units of heat?","answer":"b","hint":"The energy out must add up to the energy in.","explain":"{=a+b} − {a} = {b} units of heat. Sound plus heat equals the {=a+b} units that went in.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[6,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A ball rolls off a table and falls. Which energy grows as it falls?","choices":["kinetic energy","chemical energy","elastic energy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it getting faster or slower as it falls?","explain":"It speeds up, so its kinetic energy grows. That energy came from the gravitational energy it had up on the table."}],"explain":{"kid":["Energy makes things happen. It makes a ball roll, a lamp shine and a drum go boom. Energy has names, just like colours do.","Some energy is stored. It waits until it is needed. Food and batteries store chemical energy. A stretched band stores elastic energy. A book up on a high shelf stores gravitational energy.","Some energy is on the move. A rolling ball has kinetic energy. That is the energy of moving things. Wires carry electrical energy. The sun sends us light energy. A drum sends out sound energy. A hot cup gives out heat.","Energy can change from one kind to another. In a torch, the battery's chemical energy turns into electrical energy. The bulb turns it into light and heat. The energy is not used up. It spreads out into the room."],"adult":["This topic gives children the words for energy and energy changes in everyday things. It matches US Grade 4 standards (4-PS3-2 and 4-PS3-4), where energy is carried by sound, light, heat and electric currents and devices convert it. In England the same idea returns at Key Stage 3 as energy stores (chemical, kinetic, gravitational, elastic, thermal) and the ways energy is transferred between them.","The useful split is stored energy versus energy on the move. A battery, food, a stretched spring or a lifted object hold energy in a store. Moving objects, electric current, light, sound and heating move energy from place to place. Scientists say energy is transferred or transformed, never made or destroyed. Total energy before a change equals the total after it.","Common mistakes: children often think energy gets used up, or that a bulb or a battery makes energy. Many think only moving or living things have energy, so a still spring or a lifted book has none. Some think a battery is full of electricity rather than chemical energy. Many forget the heat and sound that every real device also gives out, even an LED.","In the lab your child sorts six things into stored and on the move, and fills in a torch chain. Next they share 10 energy blocks between light and heat for an old bulb, and see that none vanish. Last, they stretch a catapult further to send a ball further, and drop a ball from higher shelves for a bigger thump."]},"familyTip":"Go round your home and play 'energy in, energy out'. For a kettle, a radio, a toaster or a wind-up toy, say which energy goes in and which comes out (electrical in, heat and sound out). Then feel a phone charger or a lamp that has been on: the warmth is energy that has spread out, not energy that has gone.","keyIdea":"Energy is what makes things happen, and it has names. It can be stored: chemical energy in food and batteries, elastic energy in a stretched band, gravitational energy in something lifted up. It can be on the move: kinetic energy in moving things, and electrical, light, sound and heat energy. Devices change energy from one kind to another (a torch: chemical → electrical → light and heat). Energy is never made or used up; it changes and spreads out.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Stored or on the move? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Stored energy","Energy on the move"],"items":[["A slice of toast",0],["A wound-up toy spring",0],["A rock at the top of a hill",0],["A full phone battery",0],["Music from a speaker",1],["A kicked football flying",1],["Light from a lamp",1],["Warmth from a radiator",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagename","caption":"Flagship lab: sort stored energy from energy on the move, fill in a torch chain, share 10 energy blocks, stretch a catapult and drop a ball from a shelf.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_9nxyFoYD_b":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which does NOT use electricity?","choices":["a candle","a lamp","a phone"],"answer":0,"hint":"A candle burns wax. Say why, using plug, battery or neither.","explain":"A candle gives light by burning, not by electricity. A lamp and a phone both need electricity."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Mains electricity comes from…","choices":["a wall socket","a battery inside the device","the sun only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the holes in the wall you plug into.","explain":"Mains electricity comes through wall sockets. Batteries are separate. The sun can make electricity via solar panels, but that is not what 'mains' means at home."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A remote control usually runs on…","choices":["batteries","mains electricity","steam"],"answer":0,"hint":"You open the back and put small cells in.","explain":"Most remotes use AA or AAA batteries. They do not plug into the wall."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which job uses electricity?","choices":["a fan spinning air","a broom sweeping dust","a book being read"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one has a motor?. Say why, using plug, battery or neither.","explain":"A fan's motor needs electricity to spin. A broom and a book do not."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"You find {a} plug sockets in use and {b} battery devices on. How many electrical things are on?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Count both kinds. Say why, using plug, battery or neither.","explain":"{a} mains + {b} battery = {=a+b} things using electricity.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A phone that is switched off…","choices":["still stores electricity in its battery","has no electricity at all","uses mains electricity while off"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens when you press the power button?","explain":"The battery still holds chemical energy that can become electrical energy when you switch it on."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which is a battery appliance?","choices":["a handheld torch","a washing machine","a wall lamp"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one do you carry without a cable?","explain":"A torch runs on batteries. A washing machine and a wall lamp use mains electricity."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Sam says a scooter uses electricity because it moves. Is Sam right?","choices":["No, a push scooter moves without electricity","Yes, all moving things use electricity","Yes, if it has wheels"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does a push scooter have a motor or a battery?","explain":"A push scooter moves because you push it. Only an electric scooter with a battery uses electricity."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Room A has {a} electrical things and room B has {b}. How many in both rooms?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the electrical things in room A to the ones in room B.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} electrical things in total.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which of these needs electricity to work?","choices":["a fridge","a wooden spoon","a blanket"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one keeps food cold using a motor and a plug?","explain":"A fridge plugs into the wall and uses electricity to keep food cold. A spoon and a blanket do not."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A torch usually gets its electricity from…","choices":["batteries","a wall socket","burning wax"],"answer":0,"hint":"You put small cells inside a torch.","explain":"A torch runs on batteries. A wall socket is mains electricity. Burning wax is a candle, not a torch."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which list is all mains appliances?","choices":["toaster, TV, washing machine","torch, remote, toy car","book, candle, broom"],"answer":0,"hint":"Mains means plugged into a wall socket.","explain":"A toaster, a TV and a washing machine all plug into the wall. Torches and remotes use batteries. Books, candles and brooms need no electricity."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"A kitchen has {a} plugged-in appliances and {b} battery toys. How many things use electricity?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the plugged-in ones and the battery ones.","explain":"Both groups use electricity, so {a} + {b} = {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p6","prompt":"A living room has {a} lamps on plugs and {b} battery remotes. How many use electricity?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Lamps on plugs and remotes on batteries both use electricity. Add them.","explain":"Both groups use electricity: {a} + {b} = {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Look around your home. A lamp shines. A kettle gets hot. A fan spins. A radio makes a sound. A tablet shows a picture. All of these use electricity.","Some things plug into a hole in the wall. That is called mains electricity. A fridge, a TV and a toaster use mains electricity.","Other things use batteries. A torch, a remote control and many toys run on batteries. The electricity is stored inside the battery.","A wooden spoon, a book and a blanket do not light up, heat, move, make a sound or show a screen. They do not need electricity."],"adult":["In the National Curriculum for England, Year 4 Electricity asks pupils to identify common appliances that run on electricity. This lesson is the naming step before circuits, conductors and switches. It sits beside, but does not replace, 'Using electricity safely' (mains dangers) and 'Circuit words' (components, series and parallel).","The useful split is mains versus battery, then a check that the thing actually does electrical work: light, heat, movement, sound or a screen. A wind-up toy moves without electricity. A candle gives light and heat from burning, not from a plug or a battery.","Common mistakes: thinking everything that moves uses electricity (a scooter does not), thinking a battery is the same as a plug, and listing a plug itself as an appliance. Some children think a phone 'has no electricity' when it is off. The power is still there, waiting.","In the lab your child sorts room items into uses electricity or not, then into plug or battery. They tap an appliance to name the job it does with electricity, and spot three fakes that look electrical but are not."]},"familyTip":"Walk from room to room and play 'plug, battery or neither'. Point at a lamp, a remote, a clock, a broom and a phone charger. Ask which need electricity and where it comes from. Unplug nothing that is in use; just look and talk.","keyIdea":"Many things at home need electricity to work. Some plug into a wall socket and use mains electricity. Others, like a torch or a remote, use batteries. Anything that lights up, heats, moves, makes a sound or shows a screen is using electricity. Things that do none of those, such as a wooden spoon, a book or a blanket, do not need electricity. Spotting which things use electricity is the first step before learning about circuits and using electricity safely.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it need electricity? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Needs electricity","Does not need electricity"],"items":[["A lamp",0],["A fridge",0],["A remote control",0],["A phone",0],["A wooden spoon",1],["A book",1],["A blanket",1],["A pencil",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagappl","caption":"Flagship lab: sort what uses electricity, split plug from battery, name the job each appliance does, and spot three fakes that do not use electricity.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_I2JvJ-gxSh":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Units of KE from kg and m/s are…","choices":["joules (J)","m/s only","kg only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Name the energy unit from mass and speed.","explain":"With mass in kg and speed in m/s, KE is in joules."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"To find speed from KE and mass use…","choices":["v = √(2 KE / m)","v = KE / m","v = ½ m KE"],"answer":0,"hint":"Rearrange KE = ½mv² for v carefully.","explain":"Multiply by 2, divide by m, then take the square root."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"m = {a} kg, v = {b} m/s. KE?","answer":"a*b*b/2","hint":"Square v, then multiply by half the mass.","explain":"KE = ½ × {a} × {b}² = {=a*b*b/2} J.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[2,5]},"where":"(a*b*b)%2==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Tripling speed multiplies KE by…","choices":["9","3","6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Square the scale factor on speed.","explain":"(3v)² = 9v², so kinetic energy becomes nine times as large."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Forgetting the ½ in KE = ½mv²…","choices":["doubles the energy wrongly","cancels the square","fixes the units"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does dropping the half do to the value?","explain":"Leaving out ½ makes the calculated energy twice as large as it should be."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"KE = {a} J and m = 2 kg. What is v²?","answer":"a","hint":"From KE = ½mv² with m = 2, KE = v², so v² equals KE.","explain":"½ × 2 × v² = v² = {a}, so v² = {=a}.","params":{"a":[4,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"The key skill here vs the mass–speed lab is…","choices":["calculating and rearranging KE = ½mv²","only watching a cart with no numbers","naming energy stores on a ramp"],"answer":0,"hint":"Focus on the calculation work here.","explain":"Here you calculate and rearrange with units. Cart demos and store bars live in related lessons."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"m = {a} kg at v = 2 m/s. KE?","answer":"2*a","hint":"½ × m × 2² equals 2m joules.","explain":"KE = ½ × {a} × 4 = {=2*a} J.","params":{"a":[2,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Using KE = m v instead of ½ m v²…","choices":["misses the half and the square","always still correct","converts units for free"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check both missing pieces in that wrong formula.","explain":"KE = mv drops both the factor of ½ and the square on speed."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"KE equals…","choices":["½ m v²","m × v","½ m + v²"],"answer":0,"hint":"Half mass times speed squared.","explain":"The kinetic energy formula is KE = ½ m v². Products of mass and speed, or sums, are wrong."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"m = {a} kg, v = {b} m/s. KE in J?","answer":"a*b*b/2","hint":"Square the speed, multiply by mass, then by one half.","explain":"KE = ½ × {a} × {b}² = {=a*b*b/2} J.","params":{"a":[2,8],"b":[2,6]},"where":"(a*b*b)%2==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"If speed doubles and mass stays the same, KE…","choices":["becomes 4 times as big","doubles","stays the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Remember the speed is squared.","explain":"v² means (2v)² = 4v², so kinetic energy becomes four times as large."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"m = {a} kg at 4 m/s. KE in J?","answer":"a*8","hint":"½ × m × 4² = ½ × m × 16 = 8m.","explain":"KE = ½ × {a} × 16 = {=a*8} J.","params":{"a":[2,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which is the mix-up?","choices":["doubling speed only doubles KE","doubling speed multiplies KE by 4","KE = ½ m v²"],"answer":0,"hint":"Spot the false claim about doubling speed.","explain":"Because of v², doubling speed multiplies KE by four, not two."}],"explain":{"kid":["Moving things have kinetic energy. The formula is KE = ½ m v². Mass m is in kilograms and speed v is in metres per second. The answer is in joules (J).","Work carefully: square the speed first, multiply by the mass, then multiply by one half. Example: 60 kg at 5 m/s → ½ × 60 × 25 = 750 J.","Because of the v², doubling the speed multiplies the energy by four. Tripling the speed multiplies it by nine. Mass only scales the energy once.","To find speed from energy and mass, rearrange: v = √(2 KE / m). Take the positive root for speed."],"adult":["Ages 14 to 16. GCSE Energy / NGSS HS-PS3-1: calculate and rearrange KE = ½mv² with coherent SI units. This is the calculation lesson; the mass–speed dependence demo is the related conceptual topic.","Words: kinetic energy, joule, mass, speed, square, rearrange, square root. Common mistakes: forgetting the ½; not squaring v; using KE = mv; thinking doubling speed only doubles KE; mixing grams with m/s.","In the lab the learner reveals the formula, computes 750 J, sees the four-times rule, solves for v, spots the double-speed myth, and orders the method."]},"familyTip":"Ask: a 60 kg cyclist at 5 m/s — what is KE? Then: if speed doubles, does energy double or quadruple?","keyIdea":"Kinetic energy KE = ½ m v² with mass in kg and speed in m/s, giving joules. Square the speed, keep the half, and rearrange with v = √(2 KE / m). Doubling speed multiplies KE by four because of v².","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"KE calculation idea or mix-up? Drag each clue, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["True idea","Myth / mix-up"],"items":[["KE = ½ m v²",0],["double v → 4× KE",0],["KE = m × v",1],["v = √(2 KE / m)",0],["double v → 2× KE",1],["kg and m/s give joules",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagkeq","caption":"Flagship lab: reveal KE = ½mv², calculate 750 J, show the v² four-times rule, rearrange for speed, catch mix-ups, then challenge.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_loYPGHJ8lm":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A long thin line next to a short thick line is the symbol for…","choices":["a cell","a lamp","a switch"],"answer":0,"hint":"This part gives the push that makes electricity flow.","explain":"That is a cell. Two or more cells joined make a battery. A lamp is a circle with a cross, and a switch is a line with a gap."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A closed switch is drawn…","choices":["flat, joining the gap","lifted up, with a gap","as a circle with a cross"],"answer":0,"hint":"Closed means joined, so the loop has no gap.","explain":"A closed switch lies flat and joins the gap, so the circuit is complete. A lifted switch is open."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A battery symbol in a diagram shows {n} short thick lines. How many cells make up the battery?","answer":"n","hint":"Each cell has one short thick line.","explain":"One short thick line per cell, so the battery has {n} cells.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A loop has {a} cells, {b} buzzers and {c} switches. How many components are in the diagram?","answer":"a+b+c","hint":"Add up every symbol that is not a wire.","explain":"{a} + {b} + {c} = {=a+b+c} components.","params":{"a":[1,3],"b":[1,2],"c":[1,2]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A diagram shows two cells instead of one, with the same lamp. Compared with one cell, the lamp is…","choices":["brighter","dimmer","exactly the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"More cells give a bigger push.","explain":"Two cells give a bigger push (a higher voltage), so the same lamp shines brighter."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A real circuit has two cells, a pressed switch and a buzzer, joined with curly wires. Which diagram matches?","choices":["two cells, a closed switch and a buzzer, with straight wires","one cell, a closed switch and a lamp","two cells, an open switch and a buzzer"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the cells, check the switch, and check lamp or buzzer.","explain":"Two cells, closed switch (pressed on) and a buzzer. Curly wires are drawn straight. The other diagrams have the wrong cell count, a lamp, or an open switch."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"In a single loop with a cell, a switch and a lamp, does it matter which side of the lamp the switch is on?","choices":["no, it works the same anywhere in the loop","yes, it must be next to the + side of the cell","yes, it must touch the lamp"],"answer":0,"hint":"In one loop, a gap anywhere stops the whole loop.","explain":"In one loop, the switch breaks or joins the whole circuit wherever it sits. Its place does not change what it does."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A diagram has a cell, a closed switch and a lamp, but one corner of the wire is not joined. Will the lamp light?","choices":["no, the gap breaks the circuit","yes, the switch is closed","yes, corners do not count"],"answer":0,"hint":"Follow the wire all the way round. Is there any gap?","explain":"Any gap breaks the circuit, even at a corner. A closed switch cannot help if the loop is broken somewhere else."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which is the symbol for a lamp?","choices":["a circle with a cross in it","a long thin line and a short thick line","a dome on a flat base"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a round bulb with a cross drawn through it.","explain":"A lamp is a circle with a cross. The long and short lines are a cell, and the dome is a buzzer."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A diagram shows a cell, an open switch and a lamp in one loop. Does the lamp light?","choices":["no, the open switch makes a gap","yes, open means electricity gets through","yes, but only dimly"],"answer":0,"hint":"An open switch is drawn lifted up. Is the loop joined all the way round?","explain":"An open switch leaves a gap, so the circuit is broken and the lamp is off. Closing the switch joins the gap."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A battery is made of {n} cells joined end to end. Each cell is drawn with one long and one short line. How many lines are in the battery symbol?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each cell adds two lines. Double the number of cells.","explain":"{n} cells × 2 lines = {=2*n} lines.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A circuit diagram has {a} cells, {b} lamps and 1 switch, all in one loop. How many components are there?","answer":"a+b+1","hint":"Count every symbol except the wires: cells, lamps and the switch.","explain":"{a} cells + {b} lamps + 1 switch = {=a+b+1} components. Wires join components but are not counted here.","params":{"a":[1,3],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"How are wires drawn in a circuit diagram?","choices":["as straight lines with square corners","curly, like real wires","they are left out"],"answer":0,"hint":"A diagram is a neat map, not a photo.","explain":"Wires are straight lines with square corners. The real wires may curl, but only the parts and how they join matter."}],"explain":{"kid":["A circuit diagram is a map of a circuit. It uses simple symbols, not pictures. Each part has its own symbol, so anyone can read it.","A cell is a long thin line next to a short thick line. Two or more cells joined make a battery. A lamp is a circle with a cross in it. A buzzer looks like a dome. Wires are straight lines with square corners.","A switch can be open or closed. An open switch is drawn lifted up, with a gap. The gap breaks the loop, so the lamp is off. A closed switch joins the gap, so the lamp lights.","Power only flows round a full loop. If there is a gap anywhere, the lamp stays off. Real wires bend and curl, but the diagram still uses straight lines. What matters is which parts there are, how many cells, and whether the loop is joined up."],"adult":["Ages 9 to 10. In the National Curriculum in England, Year 6 Electricity asks pupils to use recognised symbols when drawing a simple series circuit. It also links the brightness of lamps and the loudness of buzzers to the number and voltage of cells, and covers the on and off positions of switches. Year 4 Electricity has already covered complete circuits and switches.","The symbols here follow the standard forms taught in UK schools. A cell is a long thin line (the positive side) beside a short thick line. A battery is two or more cells joined end to end. A lamp is a circle with a diagonal cross. A switch is a break with a hinged line: lifted is open and off, flat is closed and on. A buzzer is drawn as a dome on a flat base. Wires are straight lines with right-angle corners. In a single loop, the position of the switch does not matter.","Common mistakes: thinking an open switch is on, as an open door lets you through. Drawing a picture of a bulb instead of the symbol. Mixing up the cell and lamp symbols. Drawing wiggly wires, or leaving gaps at the corners. Believing the switch must sit next to the positive end of the cell. Expecting a lamp to light when there is a gap somewhere else in the loop.","In the lab your child taps the symbol for each of five components. They drag a wire into a gap and see the lamp light. They tap a switch open and closed. They draw a loop with a cell, a closed switch and a buzzer by tapping slots, in any order. Last, they pick which of three diagrams matches a real circuit with curly wires, two cells, a pressed switch and a buzzer."]},"familyTip":"Look at a torch or a battery toy together. Ask your child to draw its circuit using symbols, not pictures: cell, switch, lamp, and straight wires. Then ask: what does the switch do when you press it, and how would you draw that? Swap roles: you draw a diagram with a gap, and they spot why it will not work.","keyIdea":"A circuit diagram uses standard symbols instead of pictures. A cell is a long thin line and a short thick line. A battery is two or more cells joined. A lamp is a circle with a cross. A switch drawn open has a gap, so the lamp is off; closed means joined, so the lamp lights. A buzzer is a dome. Wires are straight lines. A lamp only lights if the loop is complete.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Read each circuit. Will the lamp light? Drag each card, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["lamp lights (complete)","lamp off (broken)"],"items":[["cell, closed switch and lamp in one loop",0],["cell, open switch and lamp",1],["cell and lamp with a gap in the wire",1],["battery of two cells and a lamp, all joined",0],["lamp and closed switch, but no cell",1],["cell, closed switch, buzzer and lamp in one loop",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcdiag","caption":"Flagship lab: match five symbols, close a gap with a wire, flip a switch, draw a cell–switch–buzzer loop, and match a real circuit to its diagram.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Hah24nbToi":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does a switch do in a circuit?","choices":["It opens or closes a gap","It makes electricity","It stores electricity"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the metal strip in the lab. What did it do when you pressed it?","explain":"A switch joins or breaks the path. The cell gives the push; the switch only opens or closes the gap."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which switch is on?","choices":["A closed switch","An open switch","A switch with a gap"],"answer":0,"hint":"Closed means the metal parts touch. Is the loop whole then?","explain":"Closed means the gap is joined, so current flows. Open is off."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Where can you put one switch in a simple loop so it turns the bulb on and off?","choices":["Anywhere in the loop","Only next to the battery","Only just before the bulb"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the lab, you opened the switch in three places. What happened each time?","explain":"In a simple loop, a gap anywhere stops the current everywhere, so the switch works in any place."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"You open a switch. What happens to the current in the wire before the switch?","choices":["It stops too","It keeps flowing up to the gap","It piles up at the gap"],"answer":0,"hint":"Watch the dots in the lab when the switch opens. Which ones stop?","explain":"All the charges in the loop stop at once. Nothing flows up to the gap or piles up there."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A loop has a bulb and {a} switches in a row. All are closed. How many must you open to turn the bulb off?","answer":"1","hint":"How many gaps does it take to break a loop?","explain":"Just 1. Opening any one of the {a} switches makes a gap, and the bulb goes off.","params":{"a":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Why does a doorbell stop ringing when you let go?","choices":["A spring pushes its switch open","The battery runs out","The bell gets tired"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the lab, did the doorbell button stay pressed?","explain":"A push switch is closed only while pressed. A spring opens the gap again when you let go."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Mia makes a switch with a plastic strip instead of a paper clip. The bulb never lights. Why?","choices":["Plastic does not let current through","Plastic is too light","The bulb is broken"],"answer":0,"hint":"What must the strip do when it touches both pins?","explain":"Plastic is an insulator, so it cannot close the gap. The bridge must be a conductor, such as metal."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"What happens inside the wall when you click a light switch off?","choices":["A gap opens in the circuit to the light","The electricity is stored in the switch","The bulb is unplugged"],"answer":0,"hint":"A light switch is a switch like the one in the lab, just hidden in the wall.","explain":"The switch opens a gap in the loop that runs to the light, so the current stops and the light goes off."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"A door-mat alarm buzzes when someone steps on it. What does the foot do?","choices":["Pushes two metal sheets together to close the gap","Makes electricity by squashing","Opens a gap"],"answer":0,"hint":"When does the alarm sound: when the circuit is complete or broken?","explain":"The foot squashes the mat so two foil sheets touch. The circuit is complete and the alarm buzzes."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m10","prompt":"A string of {a} fairy lights is in one simple loop with one switch. You open the switch. How many lights go off?","answer":"a","hint":"Opening the switch makes one gap. Where in the loop does the current stop?","explain":"All {a} go off. One gap stops the current all the way round the loop, so every light goes out.","params":{"a":[10,40]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"The switch in a circuit is open. What does the bulb do?","choices":["It is off","It is on","It is dim"],"answer":0,"hint":"An open switch leaves a gap. Can the current get round a loop with a gap?","explain":"Open means there is a gap, so the circuit is broken. No current flows and the bulb is off."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A loop has {a} switches in a row with one bulb. How many of them must be closed for the bulb to light?","answer":"a","hint":"Any open switch leaves a gap. How many gaps can there be if the bulb lights?","explain":"All {a} must be closed. One open switch anywhere breaks the loop.","params":{"a":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Why is the moving part inside a switch made of metal?","choices":["Current can flow through metal","Metal is heavy","Metal makes electricity"],"answer":0,"hint":"The moving part has to join the gap. What must it let through?","explain":"Metal is a conductor, so when it touches the contact, current can flow across the join."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You press a doorbell {a} times. Each time, the push switch closes and then springs open. How many times does the circuit get completed?","answer":"a","hint":"Each press closes the gap once.","explain":"{a} presses close the circuit {a} times. It opens again each time you let go.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"The switch is after the bulb in the loop. You open it. What happens?","choices":["The bulb goes off","The bulb stays on","Only half the bulb goes off"],"answer":0,"hint":"Try the switch in the 'after the bulb' box in the lab.","explain":"A gap anywhere in a simple loop stops the current all the way round, so the bulb goes off."}],"explain":{"kid":["A circuit is a loop. Current can only flow if the loop has no gaps.","A switch makes a gap and then closes it. Inside are bits of metal. When they touch, the switch is closed. The loop is whole, so the bulb lights.","When the metal bits move apart, the switch is open. Now there is a gap. The current stops all the way round, and the bulb goes off.","Open means off and closed means on. The switch can go anywhere in the loop. A light switch, a doorbell and a torch button all work like this."],"adult":["Ages 8 to 9. This is the Year 4 electricity objective in the National Curriculum in England: recognise that a switch opens and closes a circuit and link this to whether a lamp lights in a simple series circuit. Key words: circuit, complete circuit, switch, open, closed, gap, cell, battery, bulb, buzzer, wire, conductor, current.","A switch is a pair of conducting contacts that can be joined or separated. Closed, it joins the loop so current flows; open, an air gap breaks it. In a series loop the current is the same all the way round, so it stops everywhere at once when any gap opens. A switch does not store or make electricity; the cell provides the push. Push switches (doorbells, keyboard keys) spring open when released; toggle switches stay put.","Common mistakes: thinking 'open' means on, as with a door; thinking the switch must sit next to the battery or before the bulb; thinking current still flows up to the gap or in the wire before the switch; and thinking a switch makes or stores electricity.","In the lab your child presses the metal strip inside a switch, builds a paper-clip switch for a buzzer, opens one switch in three places round the loop, tries all four settings of two switches, and works a light switch, a doorbell and a door-mat alarm."]},"familyTip":"Make a switch with a paper clip, two split pins or drawing pins, a battery, a bulb or buzzer and wires on a piece of card. Then hunt for switches around the house and sort them: ones that stay where you put them (light switch) and ones that spring back (doorbell, keyboard key).","keyIdea":"A switch opens and closes a gap in a circuit. When the switch is closed, its metal parts touch, the circuit is complete and current flows, so a lamp lights or a buzzer sounds. When the switch is open, there is a gap, the circuit is broken and the current stops everywhere in the loop at once. In a simple series circuit the switch works wherever it is placed. Every switch at home, from a light switch to a doorbell, does this same job.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is the circuit complete or broken? Drag each to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Closed: the circuit is complete","Open: there is a gap"],"items":[["A doorbell button held down",0],["A torch slider pushed to off",1],["A paper clip touching both pins",0],["A paper clip swung off the pin",1],["A door-mat alarm with nobody on it",1],["A light switch clicked on",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagswitch","caption":"Flagship lab: open and close the strip inside a switch, make a paper-clip switch, move a switch round a loop, try two switches and work three switches at home.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_4RTulNwsQj":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What are the units of specific heat capacity?","choices":["J/kg °C","J/°C","kg/J"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is energy per kilogram per degree.","explain":"Specific heat capacity is joules for each kilogram and each degree, so J/kg °C. J/°C leaves out the mass."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"How many joules warm {m} kg of water from 15 °C to 25 °C? (c = 4200 J/kg °C)","answer":"m*4200*10","hint":"Find the change first: 25 − 15 °C, then multiply m × c × Δθ.","explain":"Δθ = 10 °C, so ΔE = {m} × 4200 × 10 = {=m*42000} J.","params":{"m":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"{=k*8400} J goes into 2 kg of water. How much does its temperature rise, in °C? (c = 4200 J/kg °C)","answer":"k","hint":"Rearrange: Δθ = ΔE ÷ (m × c).","explain":"Δθ = {=k*8400} ÷ (2 × 4200) = {k} °C.","params":{"k":[1,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Ella uses 70 °C instead of the 50 °C change when warming water from 20 °C to 70 °C. Her answer will be…","choices":["too big","too small","correct"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is 70 bigger or smaller than 50?","explain":"ΔE = m × c × Δθ uses the change. Using 70 instead of 50 makes her answer 40% too big."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"On a hot afternoon, why is the sand hotter than the sea?","choices":["Sand has a much lower specific heat capacity, so the same energy warms it more","The sun only shines on the sand","Sand has a higher specific heat capacity"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both get sunshine. Which needs more energy per degree?","explain":"Sand's c is about 800 J/kg °C and water's about 4200, so the same energy raises the sand's temperature much more. Mixing in the sea also spreads its energy."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Sam says copper has a low specific heat capacity, so it must be a bad conductor. What is wrong?","choices":["Conducting heat and specific heat capacity are different properties","Copper has a high specific heat capacity","Nothing is wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does how fast energy moves through a material tell you how much energy it needs per degree?","explain":"Conductivity is how quickly energy flows through a material; specific heat capacity is how much energy 1 kg needs per degree. Copper is a good conductor with a low c."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Double the mass of water and double the temperature rise. The energy needed is…","choices":["four times as much","twice as much","the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"ΔE = m × c × Δθ: what happens when two of the factors double?","explain":"Doubling m doubles ΔE, and doubling Δθ doubles it again, so the energy is 2 × 2 = 4 times as much."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A 500 g iron pan is warmed by 10 °C (c = 450 J/kg °C). Tom works out 500 × 450 × 10. What is his slip?","choices":["He used grams; the mass must be 0.5 kg","He used the wrong c","He should add, not multiply"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check the unit of mass in J/kg °C.","explain":"The value is per kilogram, so 500 g must be 0.5 kg: ΔE = 0.5 × 450 × 10 = 2250 J, not 2 250 000 J."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"1 kg of water and 1 kg of copper each get 4000 J. Which warms more?","choices":["copper","water","they warm the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which has the lower specific heat capacity?","explain":"Copper's c is 385 J/kg °C and water's is 4200, so copper needs far less energy per degree and warms about 10 °C while water warms about 1 °C."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"How much energy, in joules, warms {m} kg of water by 10 °C? (c = 4200 J/kg °C)","answer":"m*4200*10","hint":"Use ΔE = m × c × Δθ with Δθ = 10.","explain":"ΔE = {m} × 4200 × 10 = {=m*42000} J.","params":{"m":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A {m} kg aluminium block warms from 20 °C to {t} °C. How many joules did it take? (c = 900 J/kg °C)","answer":"m*900*(t-20)","hint":"Find the change first: final minus start.","explain":"Δθ = {t} − 20 = {=t-20} °C, so ΔE = {m} × 900 × {=t-20} = {=m*900*(t-20)} J.","params":{"m":[1,4],"t":[30,80]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Why do many radiators and car cooling systems use water?","choices":["Water has a high specific heat capacity, so it carries a lot of energy per degree","Water has a low specific heat capacity, so it heats quickly","Water is a good insulator"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about how much energy 1 kg of water stores for each degree.","explain":"Water's high c means each kilogram carries a lot of energy for a small temperature change, which makes it good for moving energy around."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"What does a specific heat capacity of 900 J/kg °C mean for aluminium?","choices":["900 J warms 1 kg of aluminium by 1 °C","900 J melts 1 kg of aluminium","1 kg of aluminium is at 900 °C"],"answer":0,"hint":"Read the unit: joules per kilogram per degree.","explain":"The value is the energy to warm 1 kg by 1 °C. Melting needs a different quantity, latent heat, and 900 is not a temperature."}],"explain":{"kid":["Some things heat up fast and some heat up slowly. A metal spoon in hot tea gets hot quickly. The tea in a cup cools slowly. Specific heat capacity measures this. It is the energy needed to warm 1 kg of a substance by 1 °C.","Water needs a lot: about 4200 joules for each kilogram and each degree. Sand needs about 800. So if you give 1 kg of each the same energy, the sand gets about five times hotter. A high value means it warms slowly.","To work out the energy, multiply three numbers: mass, specific heat capacity and the change in temperature. Energy = m × c × Δθ. Use the change, not the end temperature. Warming tea from 20 °C to 70 °C is a change of 50 °C.","This is why the beach sand burns your feet at noon but the sea feels cool. At night the sand cools fast, but the sea stays mild. Water is slow to warm up and slow to cool down."],"adult":["Ages 13 to 15. This is the Key Stage 3 and GCSE physics idea of specific heat capacity. It is the energy needed to raise the temperature of 1 kg of a substance by 1 °C. The equation is ΔE = m c Δθ, and it is on the AQA GCSE physics equation sheet. In the US it supports NGSS MS-PS3-4, which links energy, type of matter, mass and temperature change.","The unit is J/kg °C. You may also see J/kg K, which is the same, because a change of 1 °C is a change of 1 K. Typical values: water about 4200, ethanol about 2400, cooking oil about 2000, aluminium 900, dry sand about 800, iron 450 and copper 385. Water's value is unusually high. That is why it is used in heating and car cooling systems.","Coasts have milder weather partly for this reason. On a summer afternoon the land heats up more than the sea. At night the land cools faster. The sea also mixes warm surface water with cold deep water, and evaporation cools it, so specific heat capacity is one key reason but not the only one. The beach graph in the lab is a sketch of the pattern, not measured data.","Common mistakes: using the final temperature instead of the change; leaving out the mass, or using grams with a value per kilogram; thinking a high value means a substance heats up quickly, when it is the opposite; and mixing it up with how well a material conducts heat. Copper conducts well and has a low value, but these are two different properties."]},"familyTip":"On a sunny day at a beach or a paddling pool, feel the sand or paving and then the water at midday, and again in the evening. Ask which changed temperature more, and why the water is slow to warm up and slow to cool down.","keyIdea":"Specific heat capacity, c, is the energy needed to raise the temperature of 1 kg of a substance by 1 °C, measured in J/kg °C. Water's is high, about 4200 J/kg °C, while dry sand's is about 800 and copper's 385, so the same energy warms sand or copper far more than water. The energy needed is ΔE = m × c × Δθ, where Δθ is the change in temperature, not the final temperature, so double the mass or double the rise needs double the energy. Because water warms and cools slowly, the sea stays cool on a hot afternoon and mild at night, which gives coasts milder weather than inland areas.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"With the same energy into 1 kg, does it warm more than water or less? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["warms more than water","warms less than water"],"items":[["copper (385 J/kg °C)",0],["dry sand (about 800)",0],["aluminium (900)",0],["cooking oil (about 2000)",0],["ethanol (about 2400)",0],["liquid ammonia (about 4700)",1],["hydrogen gas (about 14 300)",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagspheat","caption":"Flagship lab: give water, oil and sand equal energy, find c for two metals, scale energy with mass, solve with ΔE = mcΔθ, and run a beach day.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_vFT_GbkP9m":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does the voltage of a cell tell you?","choices":["How hard it pushes current round a circuit","How big the cell is","How long the wires are"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what volts measure.","explain":"Voltage is the push a cell gives. Size and wire length are different things: an AA and a D cell have the same voltage."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A bulb stamped 3 V is joined to four 1.5 V cells. What is most likely?","choices":["It blows: 6 V is twice its label","It glows normally","It stays off: it needs more cells"],"answer":0,"hint":"Work out the total push, then compare it with the label.","explain":"4 × 1.5 V = 6 V, twice the 3 V the bulb is made for. The thin wire inside gets too hot and melts."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Zak says a D cell will make his bulb brighter than an AA cell because it is bigger. Is he right?","choices":["No: both are 1.5 V, so the glow is the same","Yes: bigger cells push harder","Yes: D comes after A"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check the voltage printed on each cell.","explain":"AA, C and D cells are all 1.5 V. A D cell holds more energy, so it lasts longer, but it gives the same push."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A circuit has {n} cells of 1.5 V facing the same way and one more cell turned the wrong way. What is the total voltage in volts?","answer":"(n-1)*1.5","hint":"The turned cell cancels one of the others.","explain":"{n} × 1.5 V − 1.5 V = {=(n-1)*1.5} V. A cell facing the wrong way takes its push away.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"One bulb glows well on 2 cells. A second identical bulb is added to the same loop. What happens to the glow?","choices":["Both bulbs are dimmer","Both are just as bright","The first bulb is brighter"],"answer":0,"hint":"Two bulbs in one loop share the push.","explain":"The 3 V is now shared, 1.5 V each, so less current flows and both bulbs are dimmer."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which circuit makes a motor spin fastest? All cells are 1.5 V and face the same way.","choices":["3 cells","2 cells","1 cell"],"answer":0,"hint":"More push, more current, faster motor.","explain":"3 cells give 4.5 V, the biggest push, so the most current flows and the motor spins fastest."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What is a battery?","choices":["Two or more cells joined together","Any single cell","The bulb in a circuit"],"answer":0,"hint":"Scientists use 'cell' for one unit.","explain":"One unit is a cell. Two or more cells joined make a battery, even though we often call a single cell a battery."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"How many 1.5 V cells, facing the same way, are needed to give {v} V?","answer":"v/1.5","hint":"Divide the voltage you need by 1.5 V.","explain":"{v} ÷ 1.5 = {=v/1.5} cells.","params":{"v":[3,15]},"where":"v%3==0"}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A torch has 2 cells and its bulb is dim. Which change makes it brighter?","choices":["Add a third cell, facing the same way","Turn one cell round","Swap an AA cell for a D cell"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which change adds more push?","explain":"A third cell facing the same way adds 1.5 V of push, so more current flows. Turning a cell round takes push away, and a D cell is still 1.5 V."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"{n} cells, each 1.5 V, are joined in a line, all facing the same way. What is the total voltage in volts?","answer":"n*1.5","hint":"Add 1.5 V for every cell.","explain":"{n} × 1.5 V = {=n*1.5} V. Cells facing the same way add their pushes.","params":{"n":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Two identical bulbs share one loop with {n} cells of 1.5 V. How many volts does each bulb get?","answer":"n*1.5/2","hint":"Find the total voltage, then share it between 2 bulbs.","explain":"Total: {n} × 1.5 = {=n*1.5} V. Shared by 2 bulbs: {=n*1.5/2} V each.","params":{"n":[2,10]},"where":"n%2==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Two 1.5 V cells face opposite ways in a circuit with a bulb. What happens?","choices":["The bulb stays off: the pushes cancel","The bulb is bright: 3 V","The bulb is dim: 1.5 V"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each cell pushes from its + end. Which way does each push?","explain":"The two pushes are equal and opposite: 1.5 V − 1.5 V = 0 V. No current flows, so the bulb is off."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A buzzer is quiet with 1 cell. What happens with 2 cells, facing the same way?","choices":["It is louder","It is quieter","It sounds the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does a bigger push give more or less current?","explain":"2 cells give 3 V instead of 1.5 V. More current flows through the buzzer, so it is louder."}],"explain":{"kid":["A cell pushes electricity round a circuit. The push is called voltage, and we measure it in volts, V. One normal cell gives 1.5 V. A battery is two or more cells joined.","Put cells in a line, all facing the same way, and their pushes add up. Two cells give 3 V. A bigger push makes more current flow, so a bulb glows brighter, a buzzer is louder and a motor spins faster.","If one cell faces the wrong way, it pushes back. Two cells facing opposite ways give 0 V, so the bulb goes off. A big D cell is still 1.5 V, so it is no brighter than a small AA cell. It just lasts longer.","Parts have a number on them, like 3 V. Much more push than that can make a bulb blow. Two bulbs in one loop share the push, so you need more cells to make both bright."],"adult":["This is the Year 6 Electricity unit in the National Curriculum in England: associate the brightness of a lamp or the volume of a buzzer with the number and voltage of cells used in the circuit, and compare and give reasons for variations in how components function, including the brightness of bulbs, the loudness of buzzers and the on/off position of switches. It builds on Year 4 work on simple series circuits.","Cells in series add their voltages when they face the same way (1.5 V + 1.5 V = 3 V); a reversed cell subtracts. A larger potential difference across a component drives a larger current through it, so a lamp is brighter, a buzzer louder and a motor faster. Identical lamps in series share the supply voltage equally, which is why two lamps on two cells are each dimmer than one lamp on two cells. Cell size (AA, C, D) sets how much charge a cell can deliver over time, not its voltage.","Common mistakes: thinking a bulb is simply on or off; thinking a bigger cell must give a bigger push; ignoring the direction of cells; believing more cells is always better, when a voltage well above a component's rating can burn out a lamp filament; and confusing a cell with a battery. In class, use lamps rated a little above the supply (for example 3.5 V lamps with 2 or 3 cells) so that nothing blows."]},"familyTip":"Look at the cells in a torch or a remote control together. Read the voltage printed on each one and count them. How many volts does the device get in total? Check that every cell faces the way the + sign in the holder shows, and talk about what would happen if one were turned round.","keyIdea":"A cell gives the circuit a push called voltage, measured in volts (V); a battery is two or more cells joined. Cells joined in a line, all facing the same way, add their voltages: two 1.5 V cells give 3 V. More voltage pushes more current round the circuit, so a bulb glows brighter, a buzzer sounds louder and a motor spins faster. A cell turned the wrong way pushes against the others and cancels its share. The size of a cell does not set its voltage: AA, C and D cells are all 1.5 V. Too much voltage for a part, more than the number stamped on it, can make a bulb blow. Bulbs in one loop share the voltage, so two bulbs need twice the cells to glow as brightly as one.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does the bulb get brighter, dimmer or go out? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["brighter","dimmer","goes out"],"items":[["add a cell, same way round",0],["add a second bulb to the loop",1],["take one cell out of three",1],["turn one of two cells round",2],["open the switch",2],["add 2 cells to a 2-bulb loop",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcells","caption":"Flagship lab: add cells until a 3 V bulb glows (or blows), turn a cell round, test AA, C and D cells, power a buzzer, a motor and two bulbs.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
